from data_new import X,t,CW
X("alcohol","Alcohol (ethanol)","alc","Central nervous system depressant; GABA-A enhancer and NMDA blocker.","Beer, wine, spirits, hard seltzers, liqueurs; booze, drink.",
"Fermented drinks date back at least 9,000 years (residues in pottery from Jiahu, China). Distillation spread in medieval Europe and the Islamic world. The US banned alcohol from 1920 to 1933 (Prohibition). It remains the most widely used intoxicant and causes about 178,000 US deaths a year (CDC, 2020-2021).",
"Historically a medicine and antiseptic; today used medically only as an antidote for methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning (largely replaced by fomepizole) and to destroy tissue in certain procedures.","Used socially across most cultures.","Legal for adults (21 in the US); regulated in most countries; banned in some.",
["Alcohol's character is disinhibition: at first relaxation, talkativeness and confidence, then clumsiness, slurring and poor judgment as levels rise.","Memory blackouts can occur while the person still seems awake. Emotions swing; aggression and risk-taking increase.","Alcohol poisoning kills by stopping breathing or by choking on vomit, and mixing with opioids, benzodiazepines or other depressants is a leading cause of overdose death."],
["Effects begin within 10 to 30 minutes, faster on an empty stomach: warmth and relaxation.","As blood alcohol rises, talkativeness and confidence increase.","Peak at 30 to 90 minutes after drinking stops, with the greatest impairment.","Impairment continues as long as drinking continues.","Blood alcohol falls at a roughly fixed rate; sleepiness follows.","Hangover: headache, nausea, dehydration and anxiety.","Next-day impaired concentration; daily drinkers may have withdrawal tremor."],
{"oral":t("10-30 min","30-60 min","30-90 min","depends on drinking","hours","next morning","next day")},{"oral":"Drunk."},
"Alcohol poisoning causes vomiting, confusion, slow breathing, low temperature, seizures and death.","Labels list alcohol by volume (ABV); spirits are far stronger than beer. Counterfeit or homemade spirits can contain methanol, which causes blindness and death.",
"Alcohol is broken down by alcohol dehydrogenase into acetaldehyde, then by ALDH2 into acetate; the liver clears it at a roughly fixed rate regardless of how much was drunk.",
lohi="At lower blood alcohol, relaxation and talkativeness. Higher levels bring slurring, staggering and blackouts. Very high levels cause vomiting, unconsciousness, slowed breathing, and death; binge drinking (many drinks in a short time) is the main pattern behind alcohol poisoning.",
ext=("Alcohol is absorbed mostly from the small intestine; food in the stomach slows it, and carbonation speeds it up.","Early effects come from GABA-A enhancement and dopamine release; blood vessels widen, causing warmth and flushing, while body heat is actually lost.","At high levels, alcohol suppresses the brainstem's breathing center and the gag reflex, so vomiting while unconscious can block the airway.","Blood alcohol can keep rising for 30 to 90 minutes after the last drink, so a person who 'passes out' may still be getting worse.","The liver clears alcohol at a roughly fixed rate; coffee, cold showers and food do not speed this.","Daily heavy drinkers develop dependence; stopping suddenly can cause tremor, hallucinations, seizures and delirium tremens, which can be fatal and need medical detox."),
src=["McGovern PE. et al. Fermented beverages of pre- and proto-historic China. PNAS 2004","CDC: Deaths from Excessive Alcohol Use in the United States","NIAAA: Understanding the Dangers of Alcohol Overdose"])
X("alcohol-nonbeverage","Non-beverage alcohols (methanol, isopropyl, hand sanitizer, mouthwash)","alc","Toxic alcohols and alcohol-containing products not meant for drinking.","Methanol (wood alcohol), isopropyl alcohol (rubbing alcohol), hand sanitizer, mouthwash, 'moonshine', counterfeit spirits.",
"Methanol poisoning outbreaks from bootleg or counterfeit spirits have killed thousands worldwide, including during US Prohibition and in recent outbreaks in Asia, Africa and Latin America. In 2020 the FDA recalled hand sanitizers contaminated with methanol.","Fomepizole and dialysis treat toxic alcohol poisoning.","Swallowed by people with alcohol dependence without access to drinks, by children accidentally, and in counterfeit spirits.","Legal products; methanol-contaminated sanitizers were recalled.",
["Methanol at first feels like ordinary drunkenness, but hours later its breakdown products cause severe acid build-up, blindness and death.","Isopropyl alcohol is about twice as intoxicating as ethanol and causes severe stomach irritation and vomiting.","Children who swallow hand sanitizer or mouthwash can develop dangerously low blood sugar and unconsciousness."],
["Intoxication within 30 to 60 minutes.","Drunkenness similar to ethanol.","Ethanol-like peak; methanol's real toxicity is delayed.","Hours later, vision changes and breathing difficulty (methanol).","Severe illness 12 to 24 hours after methanol.","Permanent blindness or brain damage possible.","Requires hospital care."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","1 h","1-2 h","2-6 h","12-24 h","days","permanent harm possible")},{"oral":"Swallowed."},
"Methanol poisoning causes blindness, brain damage and death; anyone who may have drunk methanol needs emergency care even if they feel fine.","Hand sanitizers, rubbing alcohol bottles, and unlabeled spirits. Check FDA recall lists.",
"Methanol is converted by alcohol dehydrogenase into formaldehyde and formic acid, which damage the optic nerve.",
ext=("Methanol and isopropyl alcohol are absorbed quickly from the gut.","Early intoxication resembles ethanol, giving false reassurance.","Methanol's metabolite formic acid causes severe metabolic acidosis and damages the optic nerve, causing blurry 'snowfield' vision and blindness.","Isopropyl alcohol causes deep sedation, low blood pressure and stomach bleeding.","Fomepizole blocks alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing methanol's toxic breakdown; dialysis removes it.","Survivors of methanol poisoning may have permanent blindness or Parkinson-like brain damage."),
src=["FDA: Hand sanitizers with methanol (2020-2022)","StatPearls: Methanol Toxicity","CDC: Alcohol poisoning"])
